# /sim/launch_ball_at — launch the simulated projectile ball from a STATE THE
# CALLER NAMES, rather than from one the simulator samples.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The simulator already had a full projectile-ball lane —
# mjSpec freejoint sphere, physical presets, quadratic drag with Magnus lift,
# noise-sampled launches, ground-truth and noisy-camera publishers — reachable
# through `/sim/launch_ball`, which is a `std_srvs/Trigger`. A Trigger carries
# no request fields at all: the callback does not even name its request
# argument. Every launch condition therefore came from YAML plus a seeded RNG,
# so a caller could ask for "a launch" but never for "THIS launch" (D-14).
#
# That is fine for a demo and useless as a measurement substrate. Catching work
# needs to re-run one throw while something else changes, to sweep a grid of
# release states, and to compare two runs that must differ only in the variable
# under test. This service is the second path into the same writer: same body,
# same physics, same publishers, but the state is stated instead of drawn.
#
# `/sim/launch_ball` KEEPS ITS MEANING. It still samples from the configured
# distribution, and the viewer's `K` key still goes through it. This service
# does not replace it and does not disturb its RNG stream — the two paths share
# only the code that writes the freejoint.
#
# REPRODUCIBILITY IS THE POINT. No RNG is consulted here, and the writer zeroes
# the ball's warm-start and applied forces and resets its orientation to
# identity, so two calls carrying the same numbers put the ball in the same
# state bit for bit. The trajectories that follow are then bit-identical for as
# long as the ball flies free.

# Release position of the ball centre, in the WORLD frame [m]. There is no
# default worth having: the configured spawn position belongs to the sampled
# path, and silently substituting it here would answer a question the caller
# did not ask. Every component must be finite.
geometry_msgs/Point position

# Release velocity of the ball centre, in the WORLD frame [m/s]. Zero is a
# legitimate request — it drops the ball — so it is not treated as "unset".
geometry_msgs/Vector3 velocity

# Release angular velocity, in the WORLD frame [rad/s]. World and body axes
# coincide at release because the writer resets the ball's orientation to
# identity, so this is unambiguous at the moment it is applied.
#
# Spin is not decoration. With aerodynamics enabled the ball carries a Magnus
# term, so the spin a caller states here changes where the ball lands. Leaving
# it zero asks for a spinless throw, which is a different throw from an unset
# one only in that the caller meant it.
geometry_msgs/Vector3 angular_velocity

---

# True iff the ball was armed with the requested state. False means NOTHING
# CHANGED — no ball was launched, no state was written, and the previous launch
# (if any) is still in flight untouched.
#
# A refusal is deliberate, never a dropped request. A caller that believed a
# launch happened would go on to attribute whatever the ball was already doing
# to the state it asked for, which is the one failure a measurement substrate
# must not have. The refusals are: the simulator is not configured; the model
# carries no projectile ball (`projectile_ball.enabled: false`, which is the
# shipped default and is also every model that never configured one); or any
# component of any field is NaN or infinite.
bool accepted

# Human-readable outcome — the armed state on success, the reason on refusal.
string message
